	tags: while, for, vector, sum,
}
-->

<statement>
<p>
	Given a positive integer, this program returns true if the number is prime.
</p>

<tabular valign="top" top="minor">
	<col width="12%"/>
	<col width="88%"/>
	<row bottom="minor">
		<cell>Inputs:</cell>
		<cell>
			<p><pf>n</pf> (1 x 1) integer — integer value.</p>
		</cell>
	</row>
	<row bottom="minor">
		<cell>Outputs:</cell>
		<cell>
			<p><pf>result</pf> (1 x 1) number — <pf>true</pf> if <pf>n</pf> is prime. <pf>false</pf>, otherwise.</p>
		</cell>
	</row>
	<row>
		<cell>Details:</cell>
		<cell><p>We wrote a version of this algorithm in a class demo. Write an improved version that is much faster and is based on the following idea:</p></cell>
	</row>
	<row>
		<cell/>
		<cell>
			<p>
				<ul>
					<li>If one of the possible numbers is not a factor of , then neither is any multiple of that number. For example, if is not a factor of , then it is redundant to check if , are factors of .</li>
				</ul>
			</p>
		</cell>
	</row>
	<row>
		<cell/>
		<cell><p>Code this program using the following strategy:</p></cell>
	</row>
	<row bottom="minor">
		<cell/>
		<cell>
			<p>
				<ul>
					<li>Use MATLAB's true Initialize a vector of all possible factors of . I will call it possible_factors here. I will leave it up to you to decide how to initialize it.</li>
					<li>Use a while loop to repeat the following tasks as long as there are still numbers in possible_factors Check if the first number in possible_factors is a factor of n. If yes, then you know the number is not prime. If no, then delete this number and all multiples of this number possible_factors</li>
				</ul>
			</p>
		</cell>
	</row>
</tabular>

<p>
	Examples:
<program>
	<code>
		S = list_sum([2 1 3 -1])
		S = 5
		S = list_sum([-2 1 0 -8 10 9 0 3.22 9 -1 -100 9.54])
		S = -69.2400
	</code>
</program>
</p>
</statement>
